      "depth": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 19,
            "column": 4
          },
          "text": "Onboard sensor data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": {
        "line": 21,
        "column": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 21,
            "column": 1
          },
          "text": "The plugin reports only hardware data exposed by the device or OS. It does not call weather services or fetch outside temperature or humidity from the network."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": {
        "line": 23,
        "column": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 23,
            "column": 1
          },
          "text": "Android can report available sensors and common readings such as battery temperature, ambient temperature, relative humidity, pressure, light, and proximity when the hardware exposes them. Android CPU and GPU temperature are best-effort thermal-zone reads and can be omitted by restricted devices."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": {
        "line": 25,
        "column": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 25,
            "column": 1
          },
          "text": "iOS reports CPU, memory, storage, GPU, thermal state, low-power mode, and CoreMotion sensor availability. Public iOS APIs do not expose raw CPU or GPU temperature."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "location": {
        "line": 27,
        "column": 1
      },
